---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="About — Thomas Dimnet"
  description="Developer and teacher, thirteen years of refusing to choose between the two. The long version of how I ended up doing both."
  current="about"
>
  <div class="region">
    <div class="about-head">
      <div class="head-text">
        <h1 class="page">About</h1>
        <p class="altitude">
          Developer and teacher, thirteen years of refusing to choose between
          the two.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="portrait">
        <div class="frame">
          <img
            src="/assets/portrait-pixel.png"
            alt="Thomas Dimnet — pixel-art self-portrait"
            width="190"
            height="190"
          />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Self-portrait, in pixels.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <section class="mvt" style="padding-top:64px;">
      <div class="col">
        <p>
          A few years into my career, OpenClassrooms offered me a full-time
          teaching job.
        </p>
        <p>
          I'd been writing courses for them on the side for four years,
          alongside my day job as a developer. The offer was the obvious next
          step: stop splitting yourself, pick the thing you're good at, do it
          properly.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="beat">I said no.</p>
      <div class="col">
        <p>
          Not out of caution — it was the safer job, and better paid. I said no
          because taking the practice away would have taken away the thing that
          made the teaching any good. I'd already met enough people who taught
          software they'd stopped writing years ago. Their courses were fine.
          They were also slightly, unmistakably out of date, in a way the
          students could feel and the teacher couldn't.
        </p>
        <p>
          That was the first time I turned down a real opportunity to protect
          something I couldn't fully justify yet. It wasn't the last.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mvt">
      <div class="col">
        <p>Here's what I couldn't have explained at the time.</p>
        <p>
          Teaching something forces you to make it visible. You can write code
          for years on instinct — the pattern works, you reach for it, you move
          on. The moment you have to put it in front of thirty people who will
          ask why, the instinct has to become an argument. Half of what I
          understand properly, I understand because I had to teach it badly
          first.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="beat">Everything I teach, I've broken.</p>
      <div class="col">
        <p>
          I know which part of Docker people actually get stuck on because I got
          stuck there, on a Tuesday, on something real that was on fire. That's
          not a detail you can get from a book.
        </p>
        <p>
          The cost is real. Thirteen years of doing both means I've never been
          the most specialized person in the room. There's always someone deeper
          in Kubernetes, someone who's read more on cognitive science. For a
          long time that felt like a deficiency I was one good year away from
          fixing.
        </p>
        <p>
          It isn't one. It's the shape of the job. The people who need me don't
          need the deepest specialist — they need someone who can sit with the
          engineers and go deep, then turn around and make the same idea land
          for someone who started six months ago, without becoming a different
          person in between.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mvt">
      <div class="col">
        <p>Years later, I took the job I'd turned down.</p>
        <p>
          A school hired me to teach programming full-time — teenagers, five
          days a week, the exact thing I'd said no to. The difference was that I
          didn't stop building. NX Academy was running alongside it, and the
          strange part is that I've never written more code than during the
          years I taught full-time. Teaching all day left me wanting to build in
          a way that building all day never did.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="beat">Then someone taught me to teach.</p>
      <div class="col">
        <p>
          My director came out of the French public school system, trained in
          cognitive science and education. Until then I'd taught on instinct. I
          was decent at it and I couldn't have told you why any of it worked.
          She made me work on the parts that don't improvise — how you stand in
          front of a room, the rhythm of a session, what to do with a silence.
          I'd been teaching for years and I was being taught the fundamentals.
        </p>
        <p>
          The framework on my <a href="/method">method page</a> comes from there.
          I didn't invent it. It was handed to me, late, by someone who'd had it from
          the start.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then Scaleway, a year ago, where the two stopped being two. They hired
          me to build a training program: teach educational engineering to
          engineers who know how to build and don't yet know how to teach — and
          design the trainings myself, so there's something to point at. I
          train, and I train the people who will train. There's no version of
          that job where I put one of the two down.
        </p>
        <p>
          These days, when someone asks what I do, I don't have a short answer.
          Developer? Teacher? Both are true, neither is enough alone. I stopped
          looking for the word.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="facts-block">
      <div>
        <p class="col-label">Now</p>
        <p class="f">
          Lead Educational Engineer, Software &amp; Cloud<span class="yr"
            >Scaleway · Paris</span
          >
        </p>
        <p class="f">
          Founder — NX Academy<span class="yr"
            >A free, open-source school for developers, in French</span
          >
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="col-label">Before</p>
        <p class="f">CTO<span class="yr">SensCritique</span></p>
        <p class="f">
          Computer Science Teacher<span class="yr">Eden School</span>
        </p>
        <p class="f">Lead Teacher<span class="yr">OpenClassrooms</span></p>
        <p class="f">Developer Advocate<span class="yr">Datadog</span></p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  /* Page head with pixel-art portrait. */
  .about-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 32px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    padding: 72px 0 8px;
  }
  .about-head .head-text {
    max-width: 34rem;
  }
  .about-head .altitude {
    margin-top: 16px;
  }
  figure.portrait {
    margin: 0;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
  }
  figure.portrait .frame {
    border: 1px solid var(--ink);
    padding: 6px;
    background: #fff;
    box-shadow: 6px 6px 0 var(--ink);
  }
  figure.portrait img {
    display: block;
    width: 190px;
    height: 190px;
    image-rendering: pixelated;
  }
  figure.portrait figcaption {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--meta);
    font-size: 12.5px;
    margin-top: 10px;
    text-align: right;
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .about-head {
      align-items: flex-start;
    }
    figure.portrait img {
      width: 132px;
      height: 132px;
    }
  }

  /* Now / Before — factual coda, raised onto a panel so it stands apart. */
  .facts-block {
    margin-top: 100px;
    background: var(--paper-2);
    padding: 38px 40px 32px;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 36px 48px;
    max-width: 48rem;
    border-top: 3px solid var(--ink);
  }
  .facts-block .col-label {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 14px;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
    color: var(--ink);
    margin: 0 0 18px;
    padding-bottom: 10px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .facts-block p.f {
    margin: 0 0 14px;
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--ink);
  }
  .facts-block p.f:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
  .facts-block p.f .yr {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: 12px;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: var(--meta);
    margin-top: 1px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .facts-block {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 28px;
      padding: 28px 22px;
    }
  }
</style>
